e is low  enough now in some of these situations  where if patients might be able to cover  it themselves and their you know payment  programs thanks so much for those  insights sounds like good news the next  question asks do you see the value in  genotyping all individual individuals in  order to have  information available at times of  prescription and is feasible cost wide  cost wise well yes yes I actually well  it depends really I think that you know  the first thing we have to get over is  this fear that genotyping individuals is  bad and this is one of the things that  you know has had a drawback in the use  of this information because many people  don't want their genetic information  available but what is important to  understand is that the type of  information that is obtained by a  laboratory for purposes of  pharmacogenetic testing it's not it's  not basically indicating anything about  a disease process or predicting your  future health etc it's really focused on  a very targeted use and that really does  target the information and because of  that I think that more and more people  are realizing that yeah I think it is  important for us to start getting this  information into our medical record so  you know the idea that we can genotype  individuals early on in life is probably  people have proposed this at birth it  may be a little bit of a stretch at this  point but certainly when you begin to  start getting different illnesses and  you begin to start aging and requiring  more and more attention medically that  might be a good time to actually start  thinking about doing genotyping that  will put your medicine record on record  so the doctors can use it so yeah I  think it does make sense to develop  platforms they're going to be able to  take this information and use it thank  you so much for your answer appears we  have time for one last question today  this question asks would you recommend  testing for panels of pharmacogenomics  tests as opposed to selecting specific  tests based on anticipated medications  yes I really do and the reason for that  as you as I indicated earlier one needs  to understand that because the  combination of genotypes sets a balance  for how much medication may be  prescribed or which one might be  effective but knowing one or two  genotypes only is not going to be  sufficient  the most sophisticated algorithms use  panels I have found basically a work  that's done by my colleagues in my  laboratory that usually around twelve to  fourteen back tests is what's optimally  necessary it links up with databases  about drug drug interactions which can  also be brought into the algorithms and  those are the main ones around twelve to  fourteen that have the best description  in the literature in terms of their  effects so I certainly recommend that  panels be considered I think that  limiting it to just a few because you're  taking one medicine or anticipating  taking two oh really is not optimum in  terms of doing the complete balance of  how one of these genotypes might  counterbalance the effects of the other  in terms of the drug selection I think I  alluded to a few examples and and some  of those examples in the literature can  be seen by some of the references and a  little textbook that I recommend it so  yes I do recommend panels over a  specific genotypes yeah thank you  excellent well thank you dr. Valdes for  your presentation today and I would also  like to thank lab roots for making  today's educational webcast possible  before we go I'd like to let everyone  know that today's webcast will be  available for on-demand viewing through  July of 2018 you will receive an email  from lab birds letting you know when  this webcast will be available for  replay please share that announcement  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event that's all for now  thank you for joining us and we hope to  see you here again soon at laberd's  thank you and have a great day goodbye
 

